  "name": "Faroese kvæði (ballad chain-dance)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Faroese kvæði Faroe Islands ballad-and-chain-dance tradition, the canonical surviving medieval-Scandinavian narrative-ballad chain-dance form that has persisted in continuous practice on the Faroes since the medieval period. Performance configuration: a circle of dancers holding hands, moving in characteristic slow-stepping pattern, with the lead-singer (skipari) singing the narrative-stanzas while the dancer-chorus responds with refrain-lines. Repertoire centers on long narrative-cycles including the Sjúrðar kvæði (Sigurd-the-dragon-slayer cycle, the canonical Faroese epic, 200+ stanzas), and other historical, mythological, and ballad-narrative cycles in classical Faroese language. The post-1948 Faroese cultural-autonomy period under the Føroya Fróðskaparfelag (Faroese Society) consolidated institutional preservation.",
